<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivan Gundulić</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1589">1589</a>–<a href="/years/1638">1638</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivan Gundulić was a poet of the Republic of Ragusa (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>) and the leading figure of Croatian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> literature. His epic Osman celebrates the Polish victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khotyn">Khotyn</a> in 1621, and his pastoral play Dubravka praises the liberty of Ragusa. A member of the city's patriciate, he also held office in its councils.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-gundulic.jpg" alt="Ivan Gundulić" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_Gondola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ivan Gundulić, baptised Dživo Franov Gundulić, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> on 8 January 1589 into a wealthy Ragusan noble family. His father Frano was a senator and diplomat. Ivan received an excellent education, probably studying the humanities with the Jesuit Silvestro Muzio, and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>. In 1608, at nineteen, he became a member of the Great Council, and he was twice, in 1615 and 1619, knez of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konavle">Konavle</a>, an area southeast of the city.</p><p>From 1621 he held various offices in the city government. He became a senator in 1636, a judge in 1637 and a member of the Small Council in 1638. He died of a fever on 8 December 1638 and was buried in the Franciscan church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>. Had he lived longer, he would probably have been elected knez himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Gundulić began with poems and melodramas staged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, but he published only his larger works, and his early writing, which he called a brood of darkness, is lost. In 1621 he published reworkings of Psalms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and religious poems. His Tears of the Prodigal Son of 1622, in three cries named Sin, Insight and Humility, sets out sin, repentance and redemption through contrasts of life and death, purity and sin, Heaven and Hell. His pastoral play Dubravka of 1628 recalls the former glory of Dubrovnik and contains some of the best-known verses in Croatian literature, among them the lines on sweet liberty.</p><p>His main work is the epic Osman. It opens with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> reckoning with the Ottoman defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khotyn">Chocim</a> in 1621 and follows the envoys he sends to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>, the battle itself and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> enslaved under Ottoman rule. The army finally captures and executes Osman and installs Mustafa. The poem contrasts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the Turks, and what the poet saw as freedom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, continues Marko Marulić's celebration of the fight against the invading Ottoman Turks, and reminds the reader constantly of fortune's wheel and the transience of the world. The 14th and 15th of its twenty cantos were never found.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Osman was first printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> in 1826 with the two missing cantos replaced by verses of a descendant, Petar Ignjat Sorkočević-Crijević. The Illyrian movement chose Gundulić's work as a model of Croatian, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mazuranic">Ivan Mažuranić</a> composed two chapters to complete the poem. Gundulić is regarded as the Croatian national poet, and his influence on the final standardisation of the language was great.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivan Gundulić” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Renaissance and Baroque writers such as Marin Držić, author of the comedy Dundo Maroje, and <b>Ivan Gundulić</b>, whose epic Osman celebrated the Polish victory over the Ottomans at Khotyn in 1621, made Ragusan Croatian a literary language.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Republic of Ragusa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.64,18.11&amp;year=1620" aria-label="Dubrovnik on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4988 648 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ivan Gundulić"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5438 918h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5438 918h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.64,18.11&amp;year=1620">What was here in 1620? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
